0..77 Comment "// The members a data block writes, side by side, and what each arrow does. A"
78..151 Comment "// single shaft builds and a double shaft computes; a left head makes the"
152..208 Comment "// arrow invertible (`surface-lowering`, `module/data`):"
209..211 Comment "//"
212..287 Comment "//   `S : (x : IntOp) --> IntOp`   a constructor: builds a term of the sort"
288..370 Comment "//   `oper pred(x : IntOp) -> IntOp` an operation: a term former the rules compute"
371..450 Comment "//   `rule P(S(x)) ==> x`           a rule: computes its left side to its right"
451..527 Comment "//   `rule x <=> S(P(x))`           an invertible rule: one term spelled two"
528..601 Comment "//                                  ways; the order chooses the direction"
602..680 Comment "//   `loop : Base <-> Base`         an invertible cell: adds a path at a point"
681..761 Comment "//   `loop : Base --> Base`         a directed cell: not minted, refused by name"
762..843 Comment "//                                  (`pathological/identity/directed-cell.gandr`)"
844..846 Comment "//"
847..917 Comment "// A rule collapses terms the constructors build, so the type is their"
918..995 Comment "// quotient; a cell adds a path between points the constructors build, so the"
996..1071 Comment "// paths at the point are the words over it. `IntOp` is presented by rules:"
1072..1152 Comment "// `S(P(x))` is `x`. `Circle` carries a cell: `loop` is apart from `here(Base)`."
1153..1229 Comment "// The integers here carry an operation the base\'s do not, so they are named"
1230..1249 Comment "// apart from them."
1251..1328 Comment "/// The integers: two constructors that cancel, one rule written directed and"
1329..1407 Comment "/// one invertible, and an operation the rules compute. The directed rule runs"
1408..1485 Comment "/// `P(S(x))` to `x` and nothing to `P(S(x))`; the invertible rule is read in"
1486..1564 Comment "/// the direction the order admits, `S(P(x))` to `x`. The operation\'s own rule"
1565..1635 Comment "/// unfolds it to a predecessor, which the directed rule then cancels."
1636..1638 Other "@["
1639..1649 Other "normalizes"
1649..1650 Other "("
1650..1651 StringLit "\""
1651..1672 StringLit "pred(S(S(Z))) ~> S(Z)"
1672..1673 StringLit "\""
